l. The fix is to run `cleanText` on `name` too, or to restrict it to a safe character set. The `tests` group doesn't cover names, so nothing catches this.\n\n2. **`evidence-read`: the C1 controls are only partly handled (read).** The 8-bit forms U+009B (CSI) and U+009D (OSC) are removed as single characters by the final `[\\x7f-\\x9f]` class. Their payload text stays visible, for example `\\x9d52;c;…` leaves `52;c;…` as plain text. The text can't act as an escape once the introducer is gone, so this is cosmetic, not a security hole. The notes' \"strips OSC/CSI\" is slightly overstated for this case.\n\n**Verified as accurate**\n\n- `prompt-extras`: `insertBeforeMaterial` runs on the prompt before evidence is added, so evidence text containing the separator can't capture the insertion point. The rules are placed before the first separator in every stage, and the honesty addendum sits just above the separator, so \"the list above\" resolves correctly.\n- `preamble`: the preamble is prepended to the prompt (`review.ts:129,154`), so \"the rules for running things below\" is correct.\n- `docs`: \"the 8-minute budget is not enforced\". `src/agents.ts` has no timeout on `runAgent`; the only timeouts are on `auth`/`login` status probes. This matches the claim.\n- `docs`: \"the evidence goes to every stage, including bugs\". The evidence is passed to all stages in `review.ts:88`, as the docs say.\n- `flag` / `import`: the flag is wired into `stagePrompt` as described.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n- `prompt-extras`: `parts.filter(Boolean).join('\\n\\n')` plus the ternary chain in `stagePrompt` is fine. No change needed.\n- The `tests` group uses an `evidenceFile` helper that creates a fresh `mkdtemp` directory per call. That's harmless, but it leaves temp dirs behind. Minor.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n- Evidence filenames are an injection path into the prompt, not only the file contents (see mismatch 1). Add this to the risks list.\n- Evidence containing the literal separator text is not tested. It is safe because it sits inside a fence before the real separator, but the test helper's `split(SEP).length === 2` assumption would break if someone tried it.\n\nI did not re-run the test suite; the orchestrator's evidence stands for that. My only run was the scratch dry-run above.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
