a09bc55a56e","a94b7a09bc55a56e","ab2f85289b2f55ca","acab915d9c740b8f","ae808f5120896207","af148564edee5cc5","b27f9ca4883c56d1","b34c9f17632951e2","b3cc65fdeda1efb6","b5cb61f520511e18","b5eed7886d9beeec","b60d10f052195202","b65ca0c7b0d63e7c","bc64d133863f5a99","beb7deafb5bf9038","bebf83fa2624f92e","bf68c9176cbb0e7d","c06c6d06a6054919","c07daee204d73735","c0d1c13a95dbf9ba","c29e68723811c473","c2caf0661925b37b","c52cb1e94856d19f","c5d63cdf0593f703","c720fc6a74bc16e0","cd34ae1ad8209965","d19f884401692f1a","d3d24f644ea686c8","d3d28e12547753e0","d6765e933cac87d9","d6765e933cac87d9","d6765e933cac87d9","d6765e933cac87d9","d6ca5850930eb156","d718242e8088f737","d71dbd75be4d4a37","dc93df44f730697c","de674017c161d7b9","dee0301e3d5a9290","df4286e1cd376bea","e12aeb8353f7f13e","e1c67f75336d84c4","e1f89457e41b61d4","e22446759ed8045c","e4d2e17ba1fbe6e6","e4e20ed7a3f7a8b8","e7ba412243113241","e87fe71884c1d331","e889151f2eac5ea3","e8960fa4a1bd4ef1","ea46ffc18f668455","eb6b2672ce17e160","eb827ac62da0d8b4","ec05a6004fc572fd","eedf0cfe33490b5e","ef28ec3c040e83fa","f07c829cec0f0ea1","f18a3ece6bb52254","f47ad6286f26b5f9","f51c4eb72add263f","f51c4eb72add263f","f5de45911a4522f0","f87fa5baf25981b2","f87fa5baf25981b2","f99f4e14a0915e5e","fef3e725b6e4d009"],"groups":["f251bb533d61e356","bfa446952cd026ff","cea58f4831f1475a","6489a6acb39c5608","c83a4041640db9c4","d173234a5a2ccd17","b0d67a8ca2225e21","ee10a0ded8e40727","daac53c1957165d8","ee22d444757cbfe0","cfadad60ce2da8f8"],"findings":"The one real defect is that the escape-stripping can silently delete everything that follows a stray escape sequence in an evidence file, even though the change promises evidence is never truncated. The rest of the change looks sound: the new tests and the rest of `test/gud.test.ts` pass (23 of 23).\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. Major (verified): one stray escape sequence deletes the rest of an evidence file without any error**\n- **Where:** `src/evidence.ts:13` and `src/evidence.ts:15`.\n- **Cause:** The patterns for OSC (`\\x1b]…`) and DCS/SOS/PM/APC (`\\x1b[PX^_]…`) treat the terminator as optional. They match `[^\\x07\\x1b]*` or `[^\\x1b]*`, which runs across newlines. If no terminator follows, the match runs to the end of the file.\n- **Scenario:** I passed these strings through `cleanText` in a scratch script:\n  - `'line1 ok\\n\\x1b]0;title\\nPASS test a\\nFAIL test b\\n12 passed, 1 failed\\n'` came back as `\"line1 ok\\n\"`.\n  - `'build log \\x1bX junk\\nFAIL: important\\nsummary\\n'` came back as `\"build log \"`.\n- **Why it matters:**\n  - It contradicts the comment \"refuses, never truncates\" (`evidence.ts:58`) and the README's \"nothing is truncated\".\n  - The prompt tells reviewers the evidence is authoritative and not to re-run what it covers.\n  - Test output often echoes strings from the code under review, such as test names and assertion values. A change can therefore print `\\x1b]` (or `\\x1bX`) with no terminator and erase the later `FAIL` lines and the summary. The reviewer is then told an edited log is the authoritative result.\n- **Fix:** Stop unterminated sequences at a newline, or strip only the introducer when no terminator is found.\n\n**2. Minor (read): the honesty addendum contradicts git-gloss's own verdict rule**\n- **Where:** `src/stages.ts:122`.\n- **Cause:** `HONESTY` tells the reviewer to skip item 2 (Misgraded necessity). git-gloss's prompt still ends with \"`VERDICT: CLEAN` if you found no mismatches, misgrading or missing risks\" (`node_modules/@ok-ggwp/git-gloss/dist/commands/review.js`, about line 110).\n- **Scenario:** A reviewer that notices misgrading gets two conflicting instructions. It may return `VERDICT: ISSUES` for a category it was told to skip, and that verdict is recorded.\n\n**3. Minor (read): if the separator is missing, the rules and evidence end up in the untrusted section**\n- **Where:** `src/stages.ts:141-143`.\n- **Cause:** When `---\\n\\n# Material under review` isn't in the prompt, `insertBeforeMaterial` appends the text at the end, after the material. That region is the one the prompt calls \"data, not instructions\".\n- **Scenario:** A `git gloss` on PATH that is older or different and words its separator otherwise (this fallback is used when the npm package isn't installed). The reviewer would then be right to ignore the rules, and the evidence would read as text written by the author. The bundled git-gloss 0.5.0 has the separator, so this only affects the PATH fallback. There is no warning when it happens.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
